From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 04:35:28 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(movement): retain completed client prediction frames

Native 1.26.51.1 protocol 2193 packets distinguish completed fluid motion
from air gravity. At eye Y 103.4815826 a native idle-water frame reports
-0.022315647 blocks per tick, while the previous air approximation reports
-0.0996117 for the matching translated position. Preserve a negotiated
client motion sample instead of reconstructing it from rounded positions.

Consume each sample once at CLIENT_TICK_END, verify that its feet position
matches the standard Java movement stream, and reject non-finite values,
unknown fields and incompatible protocol revisions. Server state and
vehicle prediction remain authoritative; ordinary Java clients retain
their existing approximation. Preserve the sampled floor motion: native
air-floor gameplay reports -0.0784, while water-floor motion is -0.005.

Track local swimming transitions from the same completed frame, including
ViaProxy clients. The client integration sends these frames after normal
Java movement and before the tick-end packet. Keep correction handling,
auth-input construction, frame validation and event state in core.

Carry completed horizontal and vertical collision axes with every prediction
frame, including stationary frames that Java omits from position updates.
Consume them only for a matching unmounted frame outside dimension changes.
Native 1.26.51.1 wall and open-water captures distinguish collision from
grounded state and clear horizontal collision on the release frame.

Apply corrected player velocity in all poses and retain zero-velocity resets.
Java relative velocity flags previously discarded this authority outside
gliding. Corrections without usable client history must still apply rather
than disappear due to a tick window check. This preserves authoritative
position and motion; input-history resimulation remains a separate gap.

Native 1.26.51.1 protocol 2193 pool-floor jumping holds Jumping, JumpDown
and WantUp without StartJumping. The old grounded-plus-held heuristic
sends a false StartJumping on the first water-rise frame even though
the full 29-frame position and motion sequence already matches native.

Accept the actual ground-jump impulse in completed prediction frames and
use it for StartJumping. Do not infer this event from motion thresholds,
held input or grounded state when the client supplies local physics.
Use a separately negotiated revision-five channel to prevent earlier
clients from sending an incompatible layout. Keep the standard Java
approximation for connections without a completed local frame.

Evaluate the native SneakTriggerIntent posture rules in core. The pinned
1.26.51.1 executable separates swimming, crawling and sneaking flags, probes
standing/crouching/crawling block space, and uses a 0.49999997F movement
threshold when crouching space is clear. Low ceilings switch swimming to
crawling outside body water and crawling back to swimming on water entry.
Keep these decisions independent from delayed server metadata.

Revision five transports all three completed posture flags. Emit swimming
and crawling edges once per connection, and distinguish effective sneaking
from physical SneakDown/WantDown input while preserving shield blocking.
The add-on supplies collision probes and local input; core owns the rule,
wire validation and Bedrock events. Metadata history rewind and input
resimulation remain incomplete.

Bypass posture input slowdown during flight, previous swimming, or water
contact for this frame. Matching 1.26.51.1 calculator 0x1404495e0 and its
adapter 0x14669d7d0 identify WasInWaterFlagComponent as the water bypass.
The movement request writer 0x148b58fe0 copies the processed coordinates.
Carry pre-input water contact in revision 5 so core packet construction
and client physics share the rule, including the crawl-to-water frame.
Reject incompatible revisions and exercise all 128 flag combinations.

Native SneakingSystem 0x14b192c50 and callback 0x14b193600 compute
min(1F, 0.3F + equipped Swift Sneak level * 0.15F) with separate float
operations. Equipment update 0x1432760e0 reads enchantment 37 from leg
slot 2, and lookup 0x1432602c0 returns the first matching enchantment.
Use this scalar for auth input instead of fixed 0.3, including ordinary
Java clients. Read only typed Bedrock enchantment entries and preserve
water, previous-swimming and flight bypasses. The add-on shares the
same scalar for local input physics. Item-use scaling remains separate.

The native scalar producer grants the enchantment bonus only when the
prior SNEAKING or CRAWLING flag is set. The initial physical sneak press
therefore uses 0.3 before the posture transition. Apply the same previous
posture in core and local physics. Keep normalized diagonal input instead
of Java's square-speed expansion, which strict BDS rejects while sneaking.
The native input calculator normalizes before applying the sneak scalar.

Equipment update 0x1432760e0 creates SwiftSneakEnchantComponent only when
absent and removes it for nonpositive enchantment levels. Positive-to-
positive replacement retains the original component level. Share this
lifecycle in SwiftSneakState and advance equipment state even while idle.
Strict BDS produces the same retained-level behavior; do not fit physics
to a newly read level that the pinned native component has not adopted.

Native parser 0x14519b430 reads the low byte of a short enchantment ID,
defaults incorrectly typed IDs/levels to zero, and preserves entry order.
Lookup 0x1432602c0 takes the first matching entry. Share that level lookup
with valid Java enchantment translation so duplicate entries cannot give
local physics a different Swift Sneak level from the core input vector.

Retain SneakingComponent as state rather than deriving this frame's scalar
from the immediately preceding pose. Registration 0x14681da00 places
ClientInputUpdateSystem before SneakingSystem and SneakTriggerActionSystem
later in the pipeline. Consume the retained scale, produce its successor
from the previous flags, then apply the completed posture. This preserves
the initial input phase and component lifecycle without tick counters.

Exercise the shared direction helper across all sixteen cardinal-key
combinations and six native scalar values. Verify zero movement when both
axes cancel, cardinal movement when one axis cancels, and normalized
movement before the scalar is applied. Keep unrelated held flags intact.

Match native ItemUseSlowdownSystem and ItemUseSlowdownApplySystem in the
pinned 1.26.51.1 executable. The reader at 0x143fd7e10 defaults an absent
component to .35, but a present component without a modifier to 1F.
The producer at 0x14c5f5e00 suppresses the component at FLT_EPSILON from 1.
The consumer at 0x14c60f9a0 squares that modifier before multiplying each
posture-scaled direction and clears the gameplay input cache. The phase-9
reset at 0x14c610370 removes the component after each frame.

Translate native item use modifiers and vibrations into Java USE_EFFECTS
in core, including network and resource-pack definitions. Keep shield
posture slowdown separate. Revision 6 carries the actual completed item
use scale so consumption, release, or slot changes cannot reconstruct a
different scale from the new inventory state. Standard Java clients use
server USINGITEM state as a fallback. Keep native scalar calculations and
packet construction in core; the add-on only samples the translated item
component and applies it after posture input.

Port unboosted glide travel into the shared core GlideMovement calculator.
Matching 1.26.51.1 GlideMoveSystem callback 0x146667b10 reconstructs view
angles with the float lookup, applies lift and pitch exchange with separate
float operations, and damps motion before collision resolution. Slow
Falling selects gravity for this kernel even during ascent. Preserve the
native multiplication order; reassociation changes rounded velocity.

Exact native instructions and the Java calculator agree in all 4,366
controlled cases, including angle seams, vertical views, ascending and
falling motion, Slow Falling, and randomized rotations. Fixtures supply
CRT remainder, the regenerated sine table, status slots and an absent
rocket boost. Retain compact numeric regression cases in Java tests.
The add-on invokes this calculation for local Bedrock glide travel on
direct and ViaProxy sessions. Collision solving, rocket effects, full
history replay, and live flight comparisons remain separate requirements.

Decode protocol 2193 MovementEffect into retained per-actor state and a
versioned client payload. Preserve signed duration, unsigned server tick
bits, and the exact binding to an accepted completed Java frame. Advance
prediction transport to revision 7 and retain 512 tick/frame bindings.
Missing or evicted bindings never restart a boost from receipt time.
Snapshots retain original timing for late channel registration.

Port the native confirmed glide boost before drag. Callback 0x146667b10
sums the complete impulse before adding entering motion; Java and the
native function match all 8,732 controlled boosted/unboosted cases.
Target 1.26.51.1 schema and packet vtable 0x14e862850 establish packet ID
318 and field offsets. Native helper 0x142fe8990 and tick callback
0x1490daf80 match 216 admission/countdown trials without history.

The add-on consumes the shared clock and calculation on both connection
routes. Full prediction replay, speculative rocket admission, input phases,
dolphin/geyser physics, ordinary Java fallback, and live joins remain open.
No matching native client trajectory was executed in this boot.
